Looking at this problem, I need to count the number of ways to place $mn$ counters on white squares of a $2m \times 2n$ checkerboard so that no two counters occupy diagonally adjacent white squares.

## Setting Up the Graph Structure

**Coordinates.** Label rows $1,\ldots,2m$ and columns $1,\ldots,2n$. White squares satisfy $i+j$ even. Diagonal adjacency means two white squares differ by $(\pm 1, \pm 1)$.

**Transform to grid coordinates.** For each white square $(i,j)$ set $p = (i+j)/2$, $q = (i-j)/2$. Then the four diagonal moves $(i\pm1, j\pm1)$ become $(p,q) \mapsto (p\pm1, q)$ or $(p,q\pm1)$ — exactly the standard grid adjacency. So the white squares form a **grid graph** (on a diamond-shaped region) under diagonal adjacency.

**Bipartition.** The grid graph is bipartite by parity of $p+q = i$. White squares with $i$ odd (both $i,j$ odd) form one part $L$; those with $i$ even form part $R$. Each part has exactly $mn$ vertices.

**Reindex.** Write $L = \{(a,b): 1 \le a \le m,\; 1 \le b \le n\}$ (odd-odd squares $(2a{-}1, 2b{-}1)$) and $R = \{(c,d): 1 \le c \le m,\; 1 \le d \le n\}$ (even-even squares $(2c, 2d)$). Then $(a,b) \sim (c,d)$ iff $c \in \{a{-}1, a\}$ and $d \in \{b{-}1, b\}$ (within bounds).

## Reducing to Maximum Independent Sets

A valid placement of $mn$ counters is an independent set of size $mn$ in this bipartite graph $G$. The matching $\{(a,b)_L \leftrightarrow (a,b)_R\}$ is a **perfect matching**, so by König's theorem the maximum independent set has size $2mn - mn = mn$. Thus $W(m,n)$ counts the **maximum independent sets**.

**Characterization via tight sets.** An independent set $S_L \cup S_R$ with $|S_L|+|S_R|=mn$ requires $S_R \subseteq R \setminus N(S_L)$, giving $|S_R| \le mn - |N(S_L)|$, hence $|N(S_L)| \le |S_L|$. By Hall's theorem (perfect matching exists), $|N(S_L)| \ge |S_L|$. Therefore $|N(S_L)| = |S_L|$ (a **tight** set) and $S_R = R \setminus N(S_L)$ is forced. This gives a **bijection** between maximum independent sets and tight subsets $S_L \subseteq L$.

